    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study analyzes the impact of local cannabis decriminalization on drug use among high school students. Using data from the Youth Risk Behavior Survey in 13 school districts across the country, I find that local cannabis decriminalization is associated with an increase in cannabis use among high school females and a decrease among males. Specifically, past-month cannabis use among males decreased by 37.43%, while female past-month use increased by 15.25% compared to pre-treatment means. Additionally, decriminalization is associated with decreases in alcohol and other drug use among both males and females. These findings suggest that cannabis decriminalization may be an effective tool for improving health outcomes among high school students, though further research is necessary to explore gender discrepancies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Bartelt, N. (2024). The Impact of Local-Level Cannabis Decriminalization on High School Drug Use. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/yr388wr9129. https://doi.org/10.25740/yr388wr9129.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
